decisions:
The interchanges are drawn at the rail's own gauge so they read as branches, not icons:
- a 3px enamel stub;
- an 18px ring holding an 8px core, drawn with an inset collar so the core can fade (E9) when it lights as a lamp on hover or focus;
- the promise said in words at the key's end: a dotted leader to 'Plans', 'To do' or 'Ideas' in the line's enamel, in the label face, then the pictogram.
Screen readers hear ', lands on Plans'. On a phone the pictogram gives way so each key holds one line. The empty chat's starters get the same ring and stub.

Every form on its own plate is now a line, in CSS only on the existing markup. This follows the sign-in page:
- a 3px steel rail in the plate's left margin;
- an 18px steel stop level with each row's labels (boxes side by side share one stop, because each stop is positioned on the plate at its own row's height);
- the stop you are typing in lit as an E20 lamp that fades over --afterglow;
- the button row as a 26px terminus joined to the buttons by a stub.
The terminus lamp comes on only when the form is :valid, so the light says 'this can be sent now'. Settings groups draw the same rail from a ring at the group's head down through each setting, fading out at the plate's foot. Kind's placeholder now reads 'e.g. restaurant', so it no longer looks like a chosen value.

